The code statements that lead to the bug are:
```c
async_urb = usb_alloc_urb(0, GFP_ATOMIC);
...
if (ret) {
    if (ret == -ENODEV)
        netif_device_detach(pegasus->net);
    netif_err(pegasus, drv, pegasus->net,
          "%s returned %d\n", __func__, ret);
}
```
Reason:
When `usb_submit_urb()` fails, the previously allocated URB (`async_urb`) and the control request structure (`req`) are not freed before returning. The error handling path only prints a message and detaches the device if needed, but omits the necessary cleanup, causing a memory leak.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```c
if (ret) {
    if (ret == -ENODEV)
        netif_device_detach(pegasus->net);
    netif_err(pegasus, drv, pegasus->net,
          "%s returned %d\n", __func__, ret);
    usb_free_urb(async_urb);
    kfree(req);
}
```
Reason:
The added `usb_free_urb(async_urb)` and `kfree(req)` ensure that the allocated URB and request structure are properly freed when `usb_submit_urb()` fails, closing the memory leak. This matches the cleanup already performed for allocation failures earlier in the function.

